# Outbox and previews

While you develop, you want to read the mail your app sends, not deliver
it. The outbox keeps every message, and the admin area shows them as they
arrive. Previews show each of your emails built from sample data, without
running the flow that sends it.

## The outbox

`howdy/mail/outbox` is an adapter that keeps messages instead of sending
them:

- `outbox.start()` keeps them in memory, until the VM stops.
- `outbox.start_in("tmp/mail")` also writes each one to the directory,
  creating it if needed, and reads back what is already there when it
  starts.

With `start_in`, each message is an `.eml` file, which any mail client
opens, and a `.json` file the outbox reads after a restart. Add the
directory to `.gitignore`: it holds sign-in tokens and whatever else your
app sent.

Only the newest 500 messages are kept. Older ones, and their files, are
removed.

Both `start` functions link the outbox to the caller. In an app with a
[supervision tree](/docs/guides/supervision), `outbox.supervised()` and
`outbox.supervised_in(directory)` return the handle and a child to add to
the supervisor instead, so a crashed outbox is restarted: one in memory
starts empty again, and one with a directory reads its files back. While
the outbox is not running, sending to it is `mail.Unavailable`, like any
provider that is away, and `outbox.messages` and `outbox.clear` return the
same error rather than crashing the caller.

## In development

Start the outbox in your `dev/` entry point, and give the app a mailer that
uses it. Register the outbox and your previews with the [admin area](/docs/admin/overview) from
`howdy_admin` to read them at `/_howdy`:

```gleam
// dev/my_app_dev.gleam
import gleam/erlang/process
import howdy/admin
import howdy/dev
import howdy/mail
import howdy/mail/outbox
import my_app

pub fn main() {
  let db = my_app.open_database()
  let assert Ok(box) = outbox.start_in("tmp/mail")
  let mailer =
    mail.mailer(outbox.adapter(box)) |> mail.default_from(my_app.sender)

  let assert Ok(_) =
    dev.start(fn() {
      my_app.app(db, mailer)
      |> admin.mount(
        admin.new()
        |> admin.mail(box)
        |> admin.mail_previews(my_app.previews(), send_with: mailer),
      )
    })
  process.sleep_forever()
}
```

Start the outbox outside the function `dev.start` rebuilds the app from,
as here, so it survives reloads.

`admin.mail(box)` lists what the outbox keeps, and shows each message's
HTML, its plain text, its headers and its raw source. New messages appear
on their own.

The admin area is a dev dependency, so it never reaches a deployment:

```toml
[dev_dependencies]
howdy_dev = { git = "https://github.com/howdy-gleam/howdy.git", ref = "v1.0.0", path = "howdy_dev" }
howdy_admin = { git = "https://github.com/howdy-gleam/howdy.git", ref = "v1.0.0", path = "admin" }
```

In production, `my_app.app` gets a mailer with a real
[adapter](/docs/mail/adapters) instead.

## Previews

A preview is a name and a function that builds a message from sample data:

```gleam
import howdy/mail/preview

pub fn previews() -> List(preview.Preview) {
  [
    preview.new("Welcome", fn() { emails.welcome(sample_user()) }),
    preview.new("Invoice", fn() { emails.invoice(sample_invoice()) })
      |> preview.in_group("Billing"),
  ]
}
```

Previews are listed under "Emails" unless `preview.in_group` gives another
heading. The admin shows each one as it would be sent, with the mailer's
default sender, and has a button that sends it through the mailer given as
`send_with`. That mailer really sends, so point it at the outbox or at a
local SMTP server such as Mailpit, never at production.

A preview is built each time it is shown, so with `howdy dev` an edited
template shows on the next refresh. A template that crashes on its sample
data, or a message `send` would refuse, shows why instead.

`admin.mail_previews` can be called more than once, and the previews are
shown together. [Howdy Auth](/docs/auth/signing-in) has a preview for
every email it sends:

```gleam
admin.new()
|> admin.mail(box)
|> admin.mail_previews(my_app.previews(), send_with: mailer)
|> admin.mail_previews(emails.previews(auth_emails), send_with: mailer)
```

## In tests

The outbox is also the adapter for tests. Start one in memory, run the flow,
and read what it sent:

```gleam
import howdy/mail
import howdy/mail/outbox

pub fn signup_sends_a_welcome_test() {
  let box = outbox.start()
  let mailer =
    mail.mailer(outbox.adapter(box))
    |> mail.default_from(mail.address("hello@acme.test"))

  let assert Ok(_) = my_app.sign_up(mailer, "ada@example.com")

  let assert Ok(sent) = outbox.latest_to(box, "ada@example.com")
  assert sent.subject == "Welcome to Acme"
  assert sent.tags == ["onboarding.welcome"]
}
```

- `outbox.latest_to(box, email)` is the newest message with that address
  among its recipients.
- `outbox.messages(box)` is every message, newest first, as
  `Ok(messages)`.
- `outbox.get(box, id)` finds one by its id, which is the `id` of the
  `Receipt` that `send` returned.
- `outbox.clear(box)` forgets every message and deletes their files.

`messages` and `clear` are `Error(mail.Unavailable(_))` only when the
outbox is not running, and `get` and `latest_to` are `Error(Nil)` then.

What the outbox holds is the `mail.Outgoing` an adapter receives, so a test
sees the message after the checks and the mailer's defaults, exactly as a
provider would.
